{
  "dom_id": "step-q183323",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z>-1.43).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ | $\\,0.9370\\,$ | $\\,0.9382\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/37674375730f32a70d7909a7ab01f3f9c836cf9c72d16447d499af20427f022c.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z∼N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>-a)=P(Z<a)=Φ(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z>-1.43).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/c80581cf6e8c2662626f70b27b669233a7bed35637dc16771c9b4a1162830efc.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z<1.43).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/014dd9aa3e4b021ae940cb6bf4fc631cb59a88ad36978ae6d152cceae41256bd.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z>-1.43)=P(Z<1.43)=Φ(1.43)=0.9236.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
